export default {"id":"note-level-8","number":8,"title":"Effects Automation","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete Self-score Gate H: two effects plus captured automation on a clip, using only facts from Level 8.","modules":[{"id":"note-m-GH-37","code":"GH","title":"Lesson 8.4.14 — Gate H assessment","blurb":"Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete Self-score Gate H: two effects plus captured automation on a clip, using only facts from Level 8.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l-o","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Lesson 8.4.14 — Gate H assessment","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Self-score Gate H: two effects plus captured automation on a clip, using only facts from Level 8."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate H is a checklist, not an auto-grader. The academy app cannot watch Note. You play the Set and tick what is actually there. Syllabus: two FX + captured automation on a clip. Practice target from Module 8.4: one automation that defines a section change."},{"type":"prose","html":"Score each line yes/no. A no is a retry, not a fail of the course. Version-gated devices (Auto Shift, Erosion, Auto Pan–Tremolo) are optional. Main FX is optional. Sampler warp is not part of this gate."},{"type":"prose","html":"If <strong>Return to Default</strong> is grey on your automated knobs, that is evidence you captured, not a problem to “fix” for the gate."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Set you used in 8.2.10–8.4.13.","Play the clip and complete the checklist below out loud.","Launch the scene transition if you built one (8.4.12 or 8.4.13).","For each <strong>no</strong>, do the smallest fix (swap an effect, <strong>Add</strong> a line, <strong>Delete</strong> a wrong line) and replay.","Write your version number next to any skipped 8.3 device.","Stop when every required line is <strong>yes</strong>."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-14","title":"H checklist","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"H checklist","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["The clip lives on a Track Preset that shows two effect slots (<strong>FX1</strong>/<strong>FX2</strong> or <strong>Pad FX</strong>/<strong>Kit FX</strong>).","I can name both loaded effects from the nine baseline categories or a version-gated device I actually have.","Series order is correct on synths (<strong>FX1</strong> then <strong>FX2</strong>), or Pad vs Kit is correct on drums, including <strong>Send Amount</strong> if I used <strong>Pad FX</strong>.","Transport was running when I created automation.","Clip Preview shows a <strong>solid</strong> line (not only a leftover dotted line).","Automated parameters show a <strong>dot</strong> under the name.","On playback, those knobs move without me touching them.","<strong>Return to Default</strong> is grey on at least one automated parameter.","I can <strong>Insert</strong> a straight line and <strong>Delete</strong> automation from the parameter menu (name, or dial on 2.0.1+).","At least one captured move defines a section change (filter open, wet ride, or delay climb)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the minimum Gate H product? <strong>Answer:</strong> Two FX on the track plus captured automation on a clip (solid line, dot, moving knobs).","Are 2.x-only effects required? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Skip with a version-gap line if they are missing.","Who scores the gate? <strong>Answer:</strong> You. Checklist from sourced facts. No fake auto-grader."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4–7.5.1","Course syllabus Gate H","Note Release Notes for any version-gated extras you included"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play the Set once without looking at this page. If you can still point to two effects and one section-changing automation, Gate H is done."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-14</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-8-1-101","code":"8.1","title":"8.1","blurb":"8.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l8-1-1","title":"8.1.1 — Two effect slots on Track Presets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.1.1 — Two effect slots on Track Presets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.1 — Effect routing rules</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find the two audio-effect tabs on any Track Preset and state the slot limit before you swap or automate anything."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A Track Preset is one instrument plus two audio effects. The manual says this in §7.1 and again in §7.4: each preset “includes two audio effects with adjustable parameters.” Those effects sit in the two rightmost tabs of the Track Preset strip, under the preset name at the top of Instrument View."},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not get Live’s free insert chain. Note’s surface is two slots. On Drift, Melodic Sampler, and Wavetable the tabs are named <strong>FX1</strong> and <strong>FX2</strong>. On a Drum Rack they are named <strong>Pad FX</strong> and <strong>Kit FX</strong>. The count is the same; the routing is not. Lessons 8.1.2–8.1.4 teach that split."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tap an effect’s name to open the audio-effect browser and replace the factory choice. Tap <strong>Done</strong> to load the new preset onto the track. Favorites work the same way they do for Track Presets."},{"type":"prose","html":"The musical reason is simple: one slot for tone (filter, EQ, saturator) and one for space or motion (delay, reverb, chorus) is how most factory presets are built. Stay inside two slots so the Set still opens as a Note Set in Live 11.2.5+."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Note and tap <strong>+ New</strong> if you need a Set.","Tap a track header to open Instrument View.","Read the Track Preset name, then scan the tabs under it.","Tap the last two tabs and confirm they hold effects, not the instrument or the sample.","Say the tab names out loud: <strong>FX1</strong>/<strong>FX2</strong> or <strong>Pad FX</strong>/<strong>Kit FX</strong>.","Open a different instrument type and find its two effect tabs from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many audio effects does a Track Preset include? <strong>Answer:</strong> Two, in the two rightmost tabs.","Where do you open those effects? <strong>Answer:</strong> Instrument View, Track Preset strip, two rightmost tabs.","Are Drum Rack effect tabs named the same as Drift? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Drum Rack uses <strong>Pad FX</strong> and <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Drift, Melodic Sampler, and Wavetable use <strong>FX1</strong> and <strong>FX2</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets; §7.4 Audio Effects in Track Presets"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one lead track and one Drum Rack, write the two effect-tab names you see. Draw FX1 → FX2 on the lead. Do not swap effects yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-1-2","title":"8.1.2 — Melodic/synth: FX1 then FX2 series","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.1.2 — Melodic/synth: FX1 then FX2 series","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.1 — Effect routing rules</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Trace the serial path on Drift, Melodic Sampler, and Wavetable: the instrument feeds <strong>FX1</strong>, and <strong>FX1</strong> feeds <strong>FX2</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Drift, Melodic Sampler, or Wavetable presets, the two rightmost tabs are labeled <strong>FX1</strong> and <strong>FX2</strong>. Manual §7.4 is explicit about order: the effect in <strong>FX1</strong> is applied to the instrument first. That processed signal then enters the effect in <strong>FX2</strong>, which outputs the final sound."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is series, not a mixer send. <strong>FX2</strong> never hears the dry instrument unless <strong>FX1</strong> lets dry signal through. If <strong>FX1</strong> is a heavy Saturator or a tight Auto Filter, <strong>FX2</strong>’s Delay or Reverb is coloring an already-shaped tone. Swap the two effects and the same pair becomes a different sound."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drum Rack is the exception you already flagged in 8.1.1. Do not treat <strong>Pad FX</strong> / <strong>Kit FX</strong> as <strong>FX1</strong> / <strong>FX2</strong>. That routing is the next lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"The local manual names no Dry/Wet label for these slots. {type:'gap'} Individual FX knobs vary by category and preset; teach only the series order here. Factory presets often put tone first and space second so a filter or saturator still sits in front of the room."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drift, Melodic Sampler, or Wavetable track.","Tap <strong>FX1</strong>, then <strong>FX2</strong>, and read both effect names.","Play a clip or hold a pad so you hear the chain.","Swap the two effects (tap each name, pick the other category, tap <strong>Done</strong>) so the former <strong>FX2</strong> now sits in <strong>FX1</strong>.","Play the same phrase. Listen for the order change, not a new instrument.","Restore the original pair from memory, or leave the swapped pair if you prefer it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which effect hears the instrument first? <strong>Answer:</strong> The effect in the <strong>FX1</strong> tab.","What does <strong>FX2</strong> receive? <strong>Answer:</strong> The output of <strong>FX1</strong>, not the raw instrument.","Does this series rule apply to Drum Rack tabs? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Drum Rack uses <strong>Pad FX</strong> and <strong>Kit FX</strong>, covered in 8.1.3."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Audio Effects in Track Presets (FX1 then FX2)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one lead clip, A/B the same pair in both orders for two minutes. Write one sentence: which order keeps the melody clearer."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-1-3","title":"8.1.3 — Drum Rack: Pad FX vs Kit FX","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.1.3 — Drum Rack: Pad FX vs Kit FX","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.1 — Effect routing rules</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a Drum Rack, tell <strong>Pad FX</strong> (one selected pad) apart from <strong>Kit FX</strong> (the whole rack) before you turn either knob."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A Drum Rack still has two effect tabs, but they are not a serial <strong>FX1</strong> → <strong>FX2</strong> pair. Manual §7.4: the third tab is <strong>Pad FX</strong>, “an audio effect whose parameters affect individual Drum Rack pads.” The fourth tab is <strong>Kit FX</strong>, “an effect that applies to the entire Drum Rack.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Select a pad first. <strong>Pad FX</strong> follows that selection. Reverb on the snare pad does not wash the kick unless you select the kick and raise its send (next lesson). <strong>Kit FX</strong> sits after the pads and colors every hit in the rack."},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.1 also describes each pad as “a Drum Sampler instrument, one audio effect send, and one audio effect for the entire Rack.” That is the same split: per-pad send into <strong>Pad FX</strong>, plus one rack-wide effect."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The official manual does not name a Dry/Wet control on <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Hear the rack-wide effect by swapping the <strong>Kit FX</strong> preset and playing the whole kit. Do not invent Live-style return tracks."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drum Rack track and select the snare pad.","Tap <strong>Pad FX</strong>. Note the effect name and that the tab is pad-scoped.","Tap <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Note that this tab is rack-wide.","Play only the selected pad, then play several pads. Hear which tab follows the selection.","Select a different pad and return to <strong>Pad FX</strong>. Confirm the tab still belongs to the new pad.","From memory, point to which tab is per-pad and which tab is the whole kit."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which tab follows the selected pad? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Pad FX</strong> (third tab).","Which tab processes every pad in the rack? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Kit FX</strong> (fourth tab).","Is <strong>Pad FX</strong> the same as <strong>FX1</strong> on Drift? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Drift <strong>FX1</strong>/<strong>FX2</strong> are a series chain on the whole instrument. <strong>Pad FX</strong> is per pad."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Drum Rack; §7.4 Pad FX / Kit FX tabs"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put Reverb on <strong>Pad FX</strong> and a different category on <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Play kick-only, then the full kit. Write which hits pick up the pad effect."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-1-4","title":"8.1.4 — Send Amount on Pad FX","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.1.4 — Send Amount on Pad FX","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.1 — Effect routing rules</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use <strong>Send Amount</strong> on <strong>Pad FX</strong> to set how much of the selected pad hits the pad effect."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Whatever category you load into <strong>Pad FX</strong>, that tab always shows <strong>Send Amount</strong>. Manual §7.4: the parameter “adjusts how much of the effect is applied to the device in the currently selected pad.” Other knobs change with the effect category. <strong>Send Amount</strong> does not go away."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the per-pad send described in §7.1. Select the snare, raise <strong>Send Amount</strong>, and only that pad feeds <strong>Pad FX</strong>. Select the kick and you are editing the kick’s send, not the snare’s. One <strong>Pad FX</strong> device is shared; each pad has its own send into it."},{"type":"prose","html":"There is only one <strong>Pad FX</strong> slot. You cannot give the hat a Delay and the snare a Reverb at the same time. You choose one pad effect and dose it per pad with <strong>Send Amount</strong>. <strong>Kit FX</strong> still hits the whole rack after that."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not publish a numeric range or a default value for <strong>Send Amount</strong>. Learn it by ear on one pad at a time."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drum Rack and select a snare or clap pad.","Open <strong>Pad FX</strong>. Find <strong>Send Amount</strong> — it is always there.","Play the pad with <strong>Send Amount</strong> low, then raise it and play again.","Select the kick. Check <strong>Send Amount</strong> on that pad. It is a different pad’s send.","Leave the kick send low and the snare send high. Play the kit.","Recite from memory: <strong>Send Amount</strong> is always on <strong>Pad FX</strong> and follows the selected pad."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which tab always includes <strong>Send Amount</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Pad FX</strong>.","Does raising <strong>Send Amount</strong> on the snare change the kick? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The parameter belongs to the currently selected pad.","Can two pads use two different <strong>Pad FX</strong> devices at once? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. One <strong>Pad FX</strong> slot; each pad has its own send into that one effect."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (Pad FX always contains Send Amount); §7.1 (per-pad effect send)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build a dry kick and a wet snare with the same <strong>Pad FX</strong> Reverb. Keep hats almost dry. Play a two-bar beat and listen for the split."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-1-5","title":"8.1.5 — Swap effect via name → browser","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.1.5 — Swap effect via name → browser","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.1 — Effect routing rules</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Replace a Track Preset’s factory effect by tapping the effect name, picking a category and preset, then tapping <strong>Done</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You do not delete an effect slot and add another. Manual §7.4: swap the default by tapping the effect’s name. That opens the audio-effect browser. Categories are Auto Filter, Channel EQ, Chorus-Ensemble, Delay, Dynamics, Phaser-Flanger, Redux, Reverb, and Saturator. Later versions add more devices (Module 8.3). Pick a preset inside a category, then tap <strong>Done</strong> to load it onto the track."},{"type":"prose","html":"The slot stays <strong>FX1</strong>, <strong>FX2</strong>, <strong>Pad FX</strong>, or <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Only the device inside the slot changes. Series order on Drift / Sampler / Wavetable does not change when you swap. <strong>Pad FX</strong> still has <strong>Send Amount</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"The browser is the same idea as the Track Preset browser: tap a name to preview, play pads to test, tap <strong>Done</strong> to commit. If you leave without <strong>Done</strong>, you have not loaded the new effect."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not document a dedicated empty/bypass slot. To hear less effect, change the preset or lower the parameters the loaded device gives you."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on any track and tap one effect tab.","Tap the effect’s name (not a knob) to open the browser.","Open a different category from the nine baseline types.","Tap a preset name and play a pad to audition.","Tap <strong>Done</strong>. Confirm the tab now shows the new effect name.","Repeat on the other effect slot from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you open the effect browser? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the effect’s name in the <strong>FX1</strong>, <strong>FX2</strong>, <strong>Pad FX</strong>, or <strong>Kit FX</strong> tab.","When does the new effect load? <strong>Answer:</strong> When you tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Does swapping an effect change FX1→FX2 order? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You replace the device in that slot. Order stays series (or Pad/Kit on drums)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (tap the effect’s name to open the browser; tap Done)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one lead, replace <strong>FX2</strong> with Reverb, then with Delay, then back to the factory effect. Keep <strong>FX1</strong> unchanged so you hear only the swap."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-1-6","title":"8.1.6 — Effect category icons","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.1.6 — Effect category icons","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.1 — Effect routing rules</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recognize each baseline effect category by its unique icon so you can find it later in Favorites."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4 lists nine baseline categories and states that each category uses a unique icon. RN 1.2 adds that you can see device icons across instruments and effects for each preset. The icon is how you scan a Favorites list without reading every name."},{"type":"prose","html":"Baseline set from the local manual:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Auto Filter</strong> — analog-style filter with LFO and envelope modulation","<strong>Channel EQ</strong> — a basic equalizer","<strong>Chorus-Ensemble</strong> — Chorus, Ensemble, and Vibrato modes","<strong>Delay</strong> — time and filter modulation","<strong>Dynamics</strong> — combined compressor and EQ","<strong>Phaser-Flanger</strong> — Doubler, Flanger, and Phaser modes","<strong>Redux</strong> — bit crusher with jitter and filter modulation","<strong>Reverb</strong> — algorithmic space","<strong>Saturator</strong> — waveshaping with shaper curves and color modulation"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Module 8.3 adds Auto Pan–Tremolo (1.7.2), Auto Shift (2.0.1), and Erosion (2.0.1). Those icons appear only if your Note version includes the device."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent Live device names that are not in this list. Note does not host AUv3."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Tap an effect name to open the browser.","Walk the baseline categories. Match each icon to the name.","Close the browser. Open it again and name three icons before you read the labels.","Favorite one preset in two different categories (lesson 8.1.7 uses this).","Open <strong>Favorites</strong> and confirm you can tell the two icons apart.","Recite the nine baseline category names from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why do effect categories use unique icons? <strong>Answer:</strong> So you can find specific effect presets later in the Favorites list.","Name the nine baseline categories in the local manual. <strong>Answer:</strong> Auto Filter, Channel EQ, Chorus-Ensemble, Delay, Dynamics, Phaser-Flanger, Redux, Reverb, Saturator.","If you do not see Auto Shift, what should you do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong> for version. Auto Shift is 2.0.1. Skip it (8.3.5) if your build lacks it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Audio Effects Icons in Note","Note Release Notes 1.2 (device icons on presets); 1.7.2 / 2.0.1 for later devices"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Cover the names and identify five category icons. Write any icon you cannot name, then look it up once."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-1-7","title":"8.1.7 — Favorite effect presets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.1.7 — Favorite effect presets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.1 — Effect routing rules</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Heart an audio-effect preset and retrieve it from Favorites by its category icon."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: you add audio-effect presets to Favorites the same way you favorite Track Presets. Because each effect category uses a unique icon, the Favorites list stays scannable. RN 1.0.12 added search and favorite for samples and presets. Use that list as a short palette, not a second library."},{"type":"prose","html":"Favorite the preset, not the empty category. The heart is on the preset you want to recall. When you later open the browser, filter or scroll to Favorites and tap the icon you already learned in 8.1.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"Favorites do not add a third effect slot. They only speed the swap in 8.1.5. A favorite Reverb still loads into <strong>FX1</strong>, <strong>FX2</strong>, <strong>Pad FX</strong>, <strong>Kit FX</strong>, or a Main FX slot (8.1.8)."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not document a numeric cap on favorited effect presets. Do not invent one. Keep the list short enough that you still recognize every icon."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the effect browser from an effect name.","Select a Reverb preset you would reuse. Favorite it.","Favorite one contrasting preset (Saturator or Redux).","Close the browser, reopen it, and go to Favorites.","Load the Reverb favorite into <strong>FX2</strong> or <strong>Pad FX</strong> and tap <strong>Done</strong>.","From memory, open Favorites and load the other hearted preset into the other slot."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do you favorite — the category or the preset? <strong>Answer:</strong> The effect preset.","Why do icons matter in Favorites? <strong>Answer:</strong> Each category has a unique icon, so you can find a preset without reading every name.","Does favoriting add another FX slot? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It only shortlists presets for the existing two slots."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Favorite Audio Effect Presets","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (search and favorite samples/presets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Heart three effect presets you would actually use on a lead: one tone, one space, one dirt. Load each onto the same clip once."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-1-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-1-8","title":"8.1.8 — Main track FX (version-aware)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.1.8 — Main track FX (version-aware)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.1 — Effect routing rules</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find the Main channel’s two effect slots in the mixer and treat them as a version-gated, Set-wide pair — not a third pair on every track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Track Presets still have two effects each. On top of that, RN 1.0.15 added a Main channel that controls system volume and can hold up to two audio effects. RN 1.3: new Sets include default effects on the Main track. Open the mixer with the <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong> icon at the bottom of Session View. Main sits with the track faders, not inside a Track Preset strip."},{"type":"prose","html":"Main FX process the sum after the tracks. A Main Reverb is not the same as <strong>FX2</strong> Reverb on one lead. Use Main for a Set-wide polish you want on every scene, and keep per-track slots for parts that must stay dry."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.2 notes the Main FX menu stays open when you add FX devices or solo/mute other tracks. That is a mixer convenience, not extra slots."},{"type":"prose","html":"The local manual’s mixer section (§4.4) documents mute, solo, and volume sliders only. {type:'gap'} Exact tap targets for adding or swapping Main FX are not written in the local manual. Use the RN facts: Main exists from 1.0.15, defaults appear on new Sets from 1.3, maximum two effects. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong> if your mixer shows no Main FX."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note is still not a Live mix. You do not get sends, groups, or more than two Main effects."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and write the installed Note version.","Open a Set and tap <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong>.","Find the Main channel (system volume, RN 1.0.15).","Look for up to two Main effects. On 1.3+ new Sets, defaults are already there.","Play a clip, then mute a track with <strong>M</strong> so you hear Main versus a single track.","From memory, say the two limits: two effects per Track Preset, two effects on Main."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When did Main FX appear? <strong>Answer:</strong> RN 1.0.15 — Main channel plus up to two audio effects. RN 1.3 — default Main effects on new Sets.","Do Main FX replace <strong>FX1</strong>/<strong>FX2</strong> on a track? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They are an extra pair on the Main sum.","How many Main effects can you add? <strong>Answer:</strong> Up to two."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Main channel + up to 2 FX); 1.3 (default Main effects); 1.2 (Main FX menu stays open)","Ableton Note Manual §4.4 Mixer (mute, solo, sliders — no Main FX text)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If your version shows Main FX, identify both slots and leave them at factory defaults. If it does not, write a <code>{type:'gap'}</code> line with your version number and skip Main for the rest of Level 8."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-1-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-8-2-102","code":"8.2","title":"8.2","blurb":"8.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l8-2-1","title":"8.2.1 — Auto Filter (LFO visualization 1.4.3+)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.1 — Auto Filter (LFO visualization 1.4.3+)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Auto Filter</strong> into an effect slot and use it as a moving filter, with the LFO visualization gated to Note 1.4.3+."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Auto Filter is “a classic analog-style filter with LFO and envelope modulation.” It arrived in Note 1.4 (“FX Device: Auto Filter is now available”). RN 1.4.3 added a real-time visualization of the LFO. If <strong>Settings → Help</strong> shows 1.4.0–1.4.2, the device exists without that display. Below 1.4, skip this lab and treat Auto Filter as missing (8.3.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Load it by tapping the effect name, choosing the Auto Filter category, picking a preset, and tapping <strong>Done</strong>. Put it in <strong>FX1</strong> when you want the filtered tone to feed Delay or Reverb in <strong>FX2</strong>. On a Drum Rack, <strong>Pad FX</strong> Auto Filter plus <strong>Send Amount</strong> can open only the hat or only the snare."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The local manual does not publish Auto Filter’s knob names or filter-type list. Do not copy Live’s Auto Filter panel. Use the knobs the loaded preset actually shows. RN 2.0.1: tapping a visual control (filter, LFO, ADSR) enters focused single-parameter mode; on iPad the visual and dials sit side by side; <strong>Done</strong> exits."},{"type":"prose","html":"A moving filter is the cheapest section change you can capture later in Module 8.4."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write your Note version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong>.","On a looping lead or hat clip, tap an effect name and load an <strong>Auto Filter</strong> preset. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Play the clip. Move the visible filter/LFO controls and listen for cutoff motion.","If you are on 1.4.3+, watch the real-time LFO visualization. If not, skip that display.","On 2.0.1+, tap the visual control once to enter focused parameter mode, then tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Bypass the experiment by swapping back to the factory effect from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the manual say Auto Filter is? <strong>Answer:</strong> A classic analog-style filter with LFO and envelope modulation.","Which version added the LFO visualization? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.4.3. The device itself arrived in 1.4.","Where do you load it? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the effect name → Auto Filter category → preset → <strong>Done</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Auto Filter","Note Release Notes 1.4 (device); 1.4.3 (LFO visualization); 2.0.1 (focused visual-control mode)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave Auto Filter on a four-bar hat or pad loop for five minutes. Find one setting that pulses with the beat and one that slowly breathes. Do not capture automation yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-2","title":"8.2.2 — Channel EQ","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.2 — Channel EQ","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Channel EQ</strong> as Note’s basic equalizer and use it to carve a part, not to “mix the song.”"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Channel EQ is “a basic equalizer.” That is the entire official description. It is one of the nine baseline effect categories. Load it like any other effect: tap the name, pick the Channel EQ category, choose a preset, tap <strong>Done</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use it when a part masks another part. A lead that honks in the same range as a vocal-like sampler, or a kit whose hats hiss over a shaker, is a Channel EQ job. Put it in <strong>FX1</strong> if you want the EQ’d tone to feed space in <strong>FX2</strong>. On drums, <strong>Pad FX</strong> Channel EQ plus <strong>Send Amount</strong> can thin one pad without touching the rack."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not name Channel EQ bands, frequencies, or a high-pass switch. Do not invent Live’s Channel EQ layout. Work only the knobs the loaded preset shows."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note still has no full mixer EQ on every channel beyond this device and the Main FX pair (8.1.8). Keep the musical idea first (Level 0.4). EQ is a clarity tool, not a reason to stall the sketch."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Loop a clip that feels crowded against another track.","Tap an effect name and load a <strong>Channel EQ</strong> preset. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Play the clip solo (<strong>S</strong> in the mixer), then with the rest of the Set.","Move only the knobs this preset exposes until the part occupies less of the colliding range.","Double-tap a knob if you need the factory default (RN 1.0.15).","A/B by swapping back to the previous effect from memory, then reload Channel EQ if it won."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the manual call Channel EQ? <strong>Answer:</strong> A basic equalizer.","How do you load it? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the effect name → Channel EQ category → preset → <strong>Done</strong>.","Are band frequencies documented in the local manual? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. {type:'gap'} Use the preset’s visible knobs only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Channel EQ","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (double-tap reset)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"EQ one looping lead so it sits next to a Drum Rack without changing the lead notes. Spend no more than eight minutes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-3","title":"8.2.3 — Chorus-Ensemble (Chorus / Ensemble / Vibrato)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.3 — Chorus-Ensemble (Chorus / Ensemble / Vibrato)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Chorus-Ensemble</strong> and hear its three documented modes: Chorus, Ensemble, and Vibrato."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Chorus-Ensemble is “a chorus effect with Chorus, Ensemble, and Vibrato modes.” That is the official mode list. Load the category, then try more than one preset so you hear all three characters: Chorus for width, Ensemble for a thicker multi-voice wash, Vibrato for pitch wobble."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 2.0.1 added new Chorus-Ensemble presets and updated existing ones to use new <strong>Tap</strong> and <strong>Time</strong> controls. If your version is below 2.0.1, you still get the three modes; you may not see those newer Tap/Time controls."},{"type":"prose","html":"Put Chorus-Ensemble in <strong>FX2</strong> after a dry <strong>FX1</strong> when you want the instrument’s tone intact and the motion on top. On a Drum Rack, a little <strong>Pad FX</strong> chorus on a clap is a different job from <strong>Kit FX</strong> chorus on the whole kit."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not define what Tap versus Time do, or list every knob. Hear the three modes. Do not import Live Chorus-Ensemble labels that Note never names."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a sustained Drift or Wavetable clip (pads show mode differences clearly).","Load <strong>Chorus-Ensemble</strong> into <strong>FX2</strong>. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Audition a Chorus preset, an Ensemble preset, and a Vibrato preset. Play the same notes each time.","If you are on 2.0.1+, look for the updated <strong>Tap</strong> and <strong>Time</strong> controls on the newer presets.","Pick the one mode that still leaves the melody readable.","Name the three modes from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What three modes does the manual name? <strong>Answer:</strong> Chorus, Ensemble, and Vibrato.","What did RN 2.0.1 change? <strong>Answer:</strong> New presets, plus existing presets updated to new Tap and Time controls.","Does missing Tap/Time mean you lack Chorus-Ensemble? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The category is in the baseline manual. Tap/Time is a 2.0.1 update."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Chorus-Ensemble","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (new presets; Tap and Time controls)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one pad clip, save a one-line note: which mode you would keep for a verse versus a chorus. Do not automate yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-4","title":"8.2.4 — Delay (time, filter, LFO options updates)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.4 — Delay (time, filter, LFO options updates)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Delay</strong> as a time-and-filter effect, then version-gate the documented Time/Feedback layout and LFO-option updates."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Delay is “a delay effect with time and filter modulation.” That is the baseline. You load it like any category. A short slap keeps a lead in place; longer time plus feedback turns the same clip into a transition (you will ride feedback in 8.4.13)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Version notes you can teach:"},{"type":"list","items":["RN 1.2.1: <strong>Time</strong> and <strong>Feedback</strong> now show before the filter section.","RN 1.2: new visualizations for Delay (and Reverb) filter and diffusion types.","RN 2.0.1: updated LFO parameter options; new Delay presets; existing presets updated to the new LFO options."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"If you are below 2.0.1, you still have Delay. You may not have the later LFO options. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not list those LFO option names. Use whatever LFO controls the loaded preset shows."},{"type":"prose","html":"Series order matters. Delay in <strong>FX2</strong> after Saturator or Auto Filter in <strong>FX1</strong> repeats a colored tone. Delay in <strong>FX1</strong> into Reverb in <strong>FX2</strong> throws repeats into the room."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Loop a short lead or hat clip.","Load <strong>Delay</strong> into <strong>FX2</strong>. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Find <strong>Time</strong> and <strong>Feedback</strong> (on 1.2.1+ they appear before the filter section).","Raise feedback briefly, then bring it down so the loop does not pile up.","If you are on 2.0.1+, open a newer Delay preset and look at the updated LFO options. If not, skip that step.","Swap Delay to <strong>FX1</strong> and put Reverb in <strong>FX2</strong>. Listen to the order, then restore the useful pair from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two kinds of modulation does the manual name for Delay? <strong>Answer:</strong> Time and filter modulation.","What did 2.0.1 change? <strong>Answer:</strong> Updated LFO parameter options, plus new and updated Delay presets that use them.","Where do Time and Feedback sit from 1.2.1 on? <strong>Answer:</strong> Before the filter section."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Delay","Note Release Notes 1.2 (visualizations); 1.2.1 (Time/Feedback before filter); 2.0.1 (LFO options and presets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set a musical delay on one clip that you can still count. Write Time/Feedback as “short slap” or “long trail.” Leave it loaded for 8.4.13."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-5","title":"8.2.5 — Dynamics (+ compressor special presets)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.5 — Dynamics (+ compressor special presets)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Dynamics</strong> as Note’s combined compressor-and-EQ effect, and find the special compressor presets added in 1.2."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Dynamics is “a combined compressor and EQ effect.” That combined device is the baseline category. RN 1.2 added “new special presets for Dynamics that contain a Compressor device.” Those special presets are how you get a more compressor-forward color inside the same category — not a second Dynamics slot."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use Dynamics when a part jumps out or disappears between hits: a sampler stab, a Drift bass, a snare in <strong>Pad FX</strong>. It is not Live’s Glue or Compressor with a full sidechain page. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not name Dynamics knobs, ratios, or the special preset titles. Browse the Dynamics category and look for the compressor-oriented presets; teach only what you can see on your version."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Drift / Sampler / Wavetable, Dynamics in <strong>FX1</strong> settles the tone before space in <strong>FX2</strong>. On a Drum Rack, <strong>Kit FX</strong> Dynamics treats the whole kit; <strong>Pad FX</strong> Dynamics plus <strong>Send Amount</strong> treats one pad."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Loop a clip with uneven hits (snare, stab, or bass).","Tap the effect name and open the <strong>Dynamics</strong> category.","Load a standard Dynamics preset. Play. Then browse for a special compressor-containing preset (1.2+).","A/B those two presets on the same clip. Tap <strong>Done</strong> on the one that controls peaks without flattening the part.","Double-tap any knob you overshot (RN 1.0.15).","Name from memory: Dynamics = compressor + EQ; 1.2 added special compressor presets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is Dynamics in the manual? <strong>Answer:</strong> A combined compressor and EQ effect.","What did RN 1.2 add? <strong>Answer:</strong> Special Dynamics presets that contain a Compressor device.","Does that add a third effect slot? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is still one Dynamics category inside an existing slot."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Dynamics","Note Release Notes 1.2 (special Dynamics/Compressor presets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put Dynamics on a bass or snare clip for five minutes. Keep only the preset that still has punch when the rest of the Set plays."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-6","title":"8.2.6 — Phaser-Flanger (Doubler / Flanger / Phaser)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.6 — Phaser-Flanger (Doubler / Flanger / Phaser)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Phaser-Flanger</strong> and hear its three documented modes: Doubler, Flanger, and Phaser."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Phaser-Flanger is “a phaser effect with Doubler, Flanger, and Phaser modes.” Load the category and switch presets until you have heard all three. Doubler is the thicker doubled image. Flanger is the jet/comb motion. Phaser is the sweeping notches."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is motion, not space. If the clip already has Chorus-Ensemble, stacking both often blurs the part. Prefer one motion device. Put Phaser-Flanger in <strong>FX2</strong> after a solid <strong>FX1</strong> tone, or on <strong>Pad FX</strong> for a single drum sound."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not list Phaser-Flanger knobs or a rate/depth pair by name. Use the controls on the loaded preset. RN 2.0.1 focused visual-control mode applies if this device shows a visual."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not confuse this category with Auto Pan–Tremolo (1.7.2) or Auto Filter (1.4). Those are different icons and different jobs."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Loop a midrange lead or a clap.","Load <strong>Phaser-Flanger</strong>. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Audition a Doubler preset, a Flanger preset, and a Phaser preset on the same notes.","Pick the mode that still leaves the rhythm readable.","Compare briefly with Chorus-Ensemble from 8.2.3, then return to one motion device.","Recite the three modes from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What three modes does the manual name? <strong>Answer:</strong> Doubler, Flanger, and Phaser.","Is Phaser-Flanger the same device as Auto Pan–Tremolo? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Auto Pan–Tremolo is a later category (1.7.2).","Where do you load it? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the effect name → Phaser-Flanger → preset → <strong>Done</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Phaser-Flanger"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep one Phaser-Flanger mode on a four-bar loop. Write which mode you would use for a breakdown versus a hook."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-7","title":"8.2.7 — Redux","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.7 — Redux","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Redux</strong> as Note’s bit crusher and use jitter/filter modulation as dirt, not as the whole arrangement."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Redux is “a bit crusher with jitter and filter modulation.” That is the official job: reduce resolution, add jitter, and let the filter move. Load it when a too-clean Drift or sampler needs grit. A little on <strong>FX1</strong> into Delay or Reverb in <strong>FX2</strong> makes the dirt repeat or bloom. A lot on <strong>Kit FX</strong> can trash the entire drum mix."},{"type":"prose","html":"Redux is not Erosion (2.0.1, noise/grit) and not Saturator (waveshaping). Different icons, different categories. If your version has Erosion, compare later in 8.3.4 instead of stacking both blindly."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not name Redux’s bit-depth, sample-rate, or jitter knobs. Work the preset in front of you. Double-tap a knob to return to the preset default (RN 1.0.15)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the sketch listenable. Beatmaking notes in the extracts tell you not to fine-tune forever. If Redux hides the hook, back it off."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Loop a clean lead or a closed hat.","Load <strong>Redux</strong>. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Increase the obvious crush/jitter until you hear aliasing, then ease it back.","Move any filter-related control the preset shows so the dirt has a tone.","Solo the track, then unsolo. If the kit or lead disappears in the Set, reduce Redux.","Swap to Saturator for ten seconds, then back to Redux, and say which dirt you kept."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the manual say Redux is? <strong>Answer:</strong> A bit crusher with jitter and filter modulation.","Is Redux the same as Erosion? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Erosion is a 2.0.1 device for noise and grit.","How do you reset a knob you overshot? <strong>Answer:</strong> Double-tap the parameter (RN 1.0.15), or use <strong>Return to Default</strong> if it is not automated."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Redux","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (double-tap reset); 2.0.1 (Erosion is a different device)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make two Redux states on one clip: “radio” and “almost clean.” Leave the almost-clean state loaded."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-8","title":"8.2.8 — Reverb","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.8 — Reverb","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Reverb</strong> as Note’s algorithmic room and place it where space belongs: usually <strong>FX2</strong>, <strong>Pad FX</strong> (snare), or Main — not on every track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Reverb “simulates how sounds echo in an acoustic space.” It is algorithmic, not a convolution library. RN 1.2 added visualizations for Reverb filter and diffusion types. Load a preset, tap <strong>Done</strong>, and treat space as a section tool. Beatmaking guidance in the extracts: a little delay and reverb helps chords linger; a piano note with long reverb can be a shot."},{"type":"prose","html":"Routing:"},{"type":"list","items":["Drift / Sampler / Wavetable: Reverb in <strong>FX2</strong> after tone in <strong>FX1</strong>.","Drum Rack: Reverb in <strong>Pad FX</strong> with <strong>Send Amount</strong> up on snare/clap only, or in <strong>Kit FX</strong> if you want the whole rack wet.","Main (1.0.15+): Set-wide wash. Easy to overdo."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The manual does not name decay, size, or dry/wet knobs for Reverb. Use the loaded preset. You will ride a wet-style parameter in 8.4.12; for now, find a static space that still leaves the attack intact."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Loop a dry chord or snare clip.","Load <strong>Reverb</strong> into <strong>FX2</strong> or <strong>Pad FX</strong>. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Play. If the attack vanishes, choose a shorter/smaller preset or lower the wet-style control you can see.","On a Drum Rack, raise <strong>Send Amount</strong> on the snare only.","If you have Main FX, compare one track Reverb versus Main Reverb, then disable the less useful one.","Recite: Reverb = algorithmic space; usually last in the chain."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Reverb simulate? <strong>Answer:</strong> How sounds echo in an acoustic space (algorithmic).","Where does it usually sit on a synth track? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>FX2</strong>, after the tone effect in <strong>FX1</strong>.","How do you wet only the snare? <strong>Answer:</strong> Reverb in <strong>Pad FX</strong>, raise <strong>Send Amount</strong> on the snare pad."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Reverb; §7.4 Send Amount","Note Release Notes 1.2 (Reverb visualizations)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (delay/reverb on chords; long-reverb shot)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build a dry verse kit and a wetter snare. Keep kick and hats almost dry. Play two scenes if you have them."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-9","title":"8.2.9 — Saturator (curves, Base bipolar)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.9 — Saturator (curves, Base bipolar)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Load <strong>Saturator</strong> as a waveshaper, hear more than one curve, and treat <strong>Base</strong> as a bipolar dial from 1.0.6 on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Saturator is “a waveshaping effect with several shaper curves and color modulation.” RN 1.2 added a new saturation curve. Browse more than one Saturator preset so you actually hear “several” curves, not one factory crunch."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.0.6: “Saturator Base dial is now a bi-polar dial.” Bipolar means the control has a center and moves both ways. Double-tap still resets a parameter to the preset default (RN 1.0.15). If <strong>Base</strong> is automated later, <strong>Return to Default</strong> greys out (Manual §7.5.1)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Saturator belongs early when you want Delay or Reverb to repeat a warm tone — <strong>FX1</strong> on a synth, or <strong>Pad FX</strong> on a kick. It is not Redux (bit crush) and not Erosion (2.0.1 noise/grit)."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not name the curve list (Analog, Soft Sine, and so on) for Note’s Saturator. Do not paste Live’s curve menu. Switch presets and listen."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Loop a bass or kick.","Load <strong>Saturator</strong> into <strong>FX1</strong> or <strong>Pad FX</strong>. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Find <strong>Base</strong>. Move it both sides of center (1.0.6+ bipolar). Double-tap to reset.","Load a second Saturator preset and compare curves.","Add a little space in <strong>FX2</strong> or <strong>Kit FX</strong> and hear the saturated tone in the room.","From memory: Saturator = waveshaping + curves + color; <strong>Base</strong> is bipolar since 1.0.6."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What kind of effect is Saturator? <strong>Answer:</strong> A waveshaping effect with several shaper curves and color modulation.","What changed in 1.0.6? <strong>Answer:</strong> The <strong>Base</strong> dial became bipolar.","What did 1.2 add? <strong>Answer:</strong> A new saturation curve."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Saturator","Note Release Notes 1.0.6 (Base bipolar); 1.2 (new curve); 1.0.15 (double-tap reset)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find a Saturator setting that makes the bass audible on phone speakers without hiding the kick. Spend under eight minutes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-2-10","title":"8.2.10 — Same clip, three FX pairs — choose winner","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.2.10 — Same clip, three FX pairs — choose winner","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.2 — Effect category labs (baseline manual set)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one unchanged clip, build three legal FX pairs — clean, dirty, space — and keep only the pair you still want after a short delay."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You now have the nine baseline categories and the two-slot rule. This lab is a decision, not a new device. Keep the MIDI notes, the instrument, and the clip length the same. Change only <strong>FX1</strong>/<strong>FX2</strong> (or <strong>Pad FX</strong>/<strong>Kit FX</strong>). That isolates the effect choice."},{"type":"prose","html":"Three sourced directions:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Clean:</strong> Channel EQ and/or Dynamics. Manual: basic EQ; combined compressor and EQ.","<strong>Dirty:</strong> Saturator and/or Redux. Manual: waveshaping; bit crush with jitter.","<strong>Space:</strong> Delay and/or Reverb. Manual: time/filter delay; algorithmic room. Beatmaking extract: delay and reverb help chords linger."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Series order is still <strong>FX1</strong> then <strong>FX2</strong>. Do not add Main FX as a third pair in this lab. Do not use Auto Shift or Erosion yet (Module 8.3). Do not automate yet (Module 8.4)."},{"type":"prose","html":"After you hear all three, put the Set down for a few minutes (Level 0.4 delay-before-judge). Keep the pair that still serves the working title."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Duplicate the track or write the factory effect names so you can return.","Load a <strong>clean</strong> pair. Play the clip twice. Note one word: clearer / smaller / dull.","Load a <strong>dirty</strong> pair. Play twice. Note one word: thicker / trashy / hidden.","Load a <strong>space</strong> pair. Play twice. Note one word: bigger / washed / stuck.","Step away for at least two minutes. Play all three again.","Leave only the winning pair loaded. Say why in one sentence."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What stays constant in this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> The clip’s notes and instrument. Only the two effect slots change.","What is the series order on a synth track? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>FX1</strong> first, then <strong>FX2</strong>.","Why wait before choosing? <strong>Answer:</strong> Beatmaking / Level 0.4: judge after a delay so you keep the memorable idea, not the last tweak."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (all nine categories; FX1 then FX2)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (delay/reverb; delay-before-judge)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Rename the Set or write a working title that matches the winning pair. You will automate that pair in Module 8.4."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-2-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-8-3-103","code":"8.3","title":"8.3","blurb":"8.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l8-3-1","title":"8.3.1 — Check version","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.3.1 — Check version","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.3 — Version-gated effects</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Read the installed Note version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and decide which Module 8.3 devices you can open."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The local <em>Ableton Note Manual</em> §7.4 lists nine effect categories. Later devices exist only in release notes. FAQ: check the version in Note at <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. Manual §2.5: the Help page also links the manual, knowledge base, and release notes, and it displays the current installation’s version number."},{"type":"prose","html":"Write the number down. Then apply these gates before you hunt for a missing icon:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>1.4</strong> — Auto Filter exists (already used in 8.2.1)","<strong>1.4.3</strong> — Auto Filter LFO visualization","<strong>1.7.2</strong> — Auto Pan–Tremolo","<strong>2.0 / 2.0.1</strong> — Auto Shift (pitch correction, vibrato, formant) and Erosion (noise/grit)"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"If a device is absent, that is a version gap, not a broken Set. Lesson 8.3.5 is the skip rule. Do not install unofficial plugins. Note cannot host AUv3."},{"type":"prose","html":"Updating Note is an App Store action (FAQ: iOS Settings → App Store automatic updates). This course does not require you to update in the middle of a lab. Record the version you have and work that surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["From the Set Library, tap the three dots and open <strong>App Settings</strong>.","Open <strong>Help</strong>.","Write the version number exactly as shown.","Tick which of Auto Pan–Tremolo, Auto Shift, and Erosion your number includes.","Open the effect browser on any track and confirm those icons match the tick list.","Recite the three version numbers from memory: 1.7.2, 2.0.1 Auto Shift, 2.0.1 Erosion."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you read the Note version? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Settings → Help</strong> (FAQ and Manual §2.5).","Which version added Auto Pan–Tremolo? <strong>Answer:</strong> 1.7.2.","What do you do if Auto Shift is missing? <strong>Answer:</strong> Treat it as a version gap and skip 8.3.3. Do not invent a substitute AUv3."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ (Settings → Help for version)","Ableton Note Manual §2.5 Help; §7.4 baseline categories","Note Release Notes 1.4 / 1.4.3 / 1.7.2 / 2.0.1"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put the version number at the top of your session notes. Circle the 8.3 lessons you will run and the ones you will skip."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-3-2","title":"8.3.2 — Auto Pan–Tremolo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.3.2 — Auto Pan–Tremolo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.3 — Version-gated effects</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If Note is 1.7.2 or later, load <strong>Auto Pan–Tremolo</strong> and hear it as a dedicated motion device — not Chorus and not Phaser."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.7.2 added a new audio-effect device: Auto Pan–Tremolo. It is not in the local manual’s nine-category list. If <strong>Settings → Help</strong> is below 1.7.2, stop and use 8.3.5. Do not pretend Phaser-Flanger is the same device."},{"type":"prose","html":"Documented follow-ups:"},{"type":"list","items":["RN 1.7.2 tweak: an icon for the waveform parameter, visible in Panning mode presets.","RN 2.0.1: refreshed Auto Pan–Tremolo UI with real-time visualization."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Official notes do not publish a full parameter list (rate, amount, shapes). Teach what you can see: Panning-mode waveform icon from 1.7.2, real-time visualization from 2.0.1. Use a factory preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"Musical use: side-to-side motion (auto pan) or level pulse (tremolo) on a pad or hat. Keep it off the kick if the groove must stay centered. One motion device is enough; if Chorus-Ensemble is already wide, A/B rather than stack."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm version ≥ 1.7.2. If not, skip this file.","On a sustained pad or closed-hat clip, tap an effect name.","Find <strong>Auto Pan–Tremolo</strong>, load a preset, tap <strong>Done</strong>.","If you see Panning-mode presets, look for the waveform-parameter icon (1.7.2 tweak).","On 2.0.1+, watch the real-time visualization. On 2.0.1+, tap a visual to enter focused parameter mode if you need it.","Bypass by swapping back to the previous effect from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When did Auto Pan–Tremolo arrive? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.7.2.","What did 2.0.1 change on this device? <strong>Answer:</strong> Refreshed UI with real-time visualization.","Is it in the local manual’s category list? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is release-note only. Manual §7.4 stops at the nine baseline effects."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.7.2 (device + Panning waveform icon); 2.0.1 (real-time visualization)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (baseline list does not include it)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave Auto Pan–Tremolo on a pad for one scene and dry on the next. Listen for motion as a section change."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-3-3","title":"8.3.3 — Auto Shift: pitch correction, vibrato, formant (2.0+)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.3.3 — Auto Shift: pitch correction, vibrato, formant (2.0+)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.3 — Version-gated effects</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+, load <strong>Auto Shift</strong> and treat it as real-time pitch correction, vibrato, and formant shifting — not as Live’s full Auto Shift suite."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"RN 2.0.1: Auto Shift is a new FX device “for real-time pitch correction, vibrato and formant shifting.” Core Library updates in the same release added Auto Shift presets. The local manual does not list this category. If <strong>Settings → Help</strong> is below 2.0.1, skip this lesson (8.3.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Load it like any effect: tap the name, find Auto Shift, pick a preset, tap <strong>Done</strong>. Put it on a Melodic Sampler vocal-like phrase or a Drift lead when you want correction or a formant color. It does not add MIDI pitch bend (FAQ: pitch bend is not available in Note)."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Official notes do not name Auto Shift knobs, scale-follow behavior, or a retune-speed control. Do not paste Live Auto Shift’s parameter set. Stay on factory presets and the three jobs the release note names: pitch correction, vibrato, formant."},{"type":"prose","html":"Audio-track warping (also 2.0.1) is a different feature. Auto Shift is an effect. Warped audio loops live in Module 9.5."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm version ≥ 2.0.1. If not, write a skip line and stop.","Open a melodic clip (Sampler or Drift).","Load an <strong>Auto Shift</strong> preset into <strong>FX1</strong> or <strong>FX2</strong>. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","Play in the Set’s key (Set Settings → Key &amp; Scale) and listen for correction, vibrato, or formant change.","Compare two Auto Shift presets, then keep the one that still sounds like your part.","Recite the three official jobs from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version added Auto Shift? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 2.0.1.","What three jobs does the release note name? <strong>Answer:</strong> Real-time pitch correction, vibrato, and formant shifting.","Does Auto Shift give you pitch-bend MIDI? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Pitch bend is not available in Note (FAQ)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 Auto Shift; Core Library Auto Shift presets","Note FAQ (pitch bend not available)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (category absent — version gap)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If 2.0.1+ is installed, keep one Auto Shift preset on a short vocal-like or lead clip. If not, skip and go to 8.3.5."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-3-4","title":"8.3.4 — Erosion: noise/grit (2.0+)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.3.4 — Erosion: noise/grit (2.0+)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.3 — Version-gated effects</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+, load <strong>Erosion</strong> as the noise-and-grit FX device and keep it distinct from Redux and Saturator."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"RN 2.0.1 added Erosion “as a new FX Device for adding noise and grit to a sound,” plus Erosion presets in the Core Library. The local manual’s nine categories do not include it. Below 2.0.1, skip (8.3.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Grit map you can defend from sources:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Saturator</strong> — waveshaping, curves, color (manual)","<strong>Redux</strong> — bit crush, jitter, filter modulation (manual)","<strong>Erosion</strong> — noise and grit (RN 2.0.1)"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not stack all three on one clip for this lab. Load Erosion in <strong>FX1</strong> if you want the noise to feed Delay/Reverb, or on <strong>Pad FX</strong> if only one drum should rasp."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Official notes do not name Erosion modes (Sine, Noise, and so on) or frequency knobs. Use a factory preset. Do not import Live Erosion’s panel."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm version ≥ 2.0.1. If not, skip.","Loop a clean hat or mid lead.","Load an <strong>Erosion</strong> preset. Tap <strong>Done</strong>.","A/B against a Redux preset on the same slot, then against Saturator. Return to Erosion.","Decide whether the noise sits better before space (<strong>FX1</strong>) or after tone (<strong>FX2</strong>).","Name the three dirt devices from memory: Saturator, Redux, Erosion."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When did Erosion arrive? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 2.0.1.","What does the release note say it adds? <strong>Answer:</strong> Noise and grit.","Is Erosion in the local manual? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Manual §7.4 is the pre-2.0 baseline list."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 Erosion; Core Library Erosion presets","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Saturator and Redux (contrast)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep Erosion only if it still lets you hear the hook. Otherwise swap back to the 8.2.10 winner."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-3-5","title":"8.3.5 — Skip effects that your Note version does not include","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.3.5 — Skip effects that your Note version does not include","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.3 — Version-gated effects</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a version-gap card for any 8.3 device your Note build lacks, and continue the course on the effects you actually have."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Missing Auto Pan–Tremolo, Auto Shift, or Erosion is expected on older builds. It is not a broken browser. FAQ: the version lives at <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. Never blend 2.x-only devices into the pre-2.0 manual surface."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this skip card, one line per missing device:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<code>{type:'gap'} Note &lt;version&gt; lacks &lt;device&gt; (needs RN &lt;version&gt;). Skipped.</code>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Examples you can copy:"},{"type":"list","items":["<code>{type:'gap'} Note 1.6 lacks Auto Pan–Tremolo (needs RN 1.7.2). Skipped.</code>","<code>{type:'gap'} Note 1.7.3 lacks Auto Shift and Erosion (needs RN 2.0.1). Skipped.</code>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not replace a missing device with an AUv3. FAQ: AUv3 is not available, because that would break Live compatibility on Windows. Do not claim Phaser-Flanger “is” Auto Pan–Tremolo. You may keep working with the nine manual categories plus whatever your version added."},{"type":"prose","html":"Module 8.4 automation works on the effects you have. Gate H does not require Auto Shift."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Read the version you wrote in 8.3.1.","List Auto Pan–Tremolo, Auto Shift, and Erosion. Mark present or missing.","For each missing device, write the <code>{type:'gap'}</code> line with the required RN version.","Open the effect browser once more and confirm you are not hunting for a hidden icon.","Return to the FX pair you chose in 8.2.10.","Recite: skip missing devices; do not invent substitutes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the version number? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Settings → Help</strong>.","May you install an AUv3 to replace Auto Shift? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. AUv3 is not available in Note.","Can you still finish Gate H without 2.0.1 devices? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Automation (Manual §7.5) runs on the baseline effects."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ (version at Settings → Help; no AUv3)","Note Release Notes 1.7.2 / 2.0.1"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave the gap card in your session notes. Start Module 8.4 with the effects your build actually loads."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-8-4-104","code":"8.4","title":"8.4","blurb":"8.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l8-4-1","title":"8.4.1 — Transport running + twist knob → dotted automation line in Clip Preview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.1 — Transport running + twist knob → dotted automation line in Clip Preview","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Start transport, move a device or effect knob, and recognize the dotted line in Clip Preview as pending automation."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5: you change device and audio-effect parameters while a clip is playing, then capture those changes so they play back automatically. That captured motion is automation. The first mechanical rule is absolute: Note’s transport <strong>must</strong> be running. Twist a knob with the Set stopped and you are only changing a static value. You will not see automation recognized."},{"type":"prose","html":"When transport is running and you adjust a parameter, “a dotted line has appeared in the Clip Preview window, indicating that automation was recognized for that parameter.” Dotted means pending. It is not stored in the clip yet. <strong>Add</strong> (next lesson) is what captures it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Clip Preview is the small clip overview in Instrument View — the same area you tap to open the MIDI Editor. Watch it while you move one knob on <strong>FX1</strong>, <strong>FX2</strong>, or a device parameter."},{"type":"prose","html":"FAQ: “Automation in Note is recorded via Capture.” The manual’s capture control for this motion is the <strong>Add</strong> button once a clip already exists. Do not confuse this with Free Length Capture of notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that already loops (notes already captured).","Press Play or launch the clip so transport is running.","Open an effect or device tab. Move one knob slowly for a bar.","Watch Clip Preview. Confirm a <strong>dotted</strong> line appeared.","Stop transport. Move the same knob. Confirm no new dotted automation is recognized while stopped.","Start transport again and reproduce the dotted line from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Must transport be running to create automation? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Manual §7.5.","What does a dotted line in Clip Preview mean? <strong>Answer:</strong> Automation was recognized for that parameter but is not captured yet.","Does twisting a knob while stopped write automation? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That only changes the static value."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 Automating Parameters","Note FAQ (automation recorded via Capture)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Produce a dotted filter or wet line on one clip. Do not tap <strong>Add</strong> yet. Leave the clip playing so 8.4.2 can capture it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-2","title":"8.4.2 — Add button captures automation into clip","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.2 — Add button captures automation into clip","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap <strong>Add</strong> while a dotted automation line is showing so that motion is stored in the clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5: after the dotted line appears, “Tap the <strong>Add</strong> button at the bottom of the app to capture the automation into the clip.” <strong>Add</strong> is the same bottom-of-Instrument-View control you already use to add notes to a Fixed Length clip. Here it commits parameter motion."},{"type":"prose","html":"Until you tap <strong>Add</strong>, the move is only recognized, not stored. Stop and relaunch without <strong>Add</strong> and you should not expect that gesture to be part of the loop. After <strong>Add</strong>, the line becomes solid (8.4.4) and a dot appears under the parameter name."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you are overdubbing notes and automation in the same pass, <strong>Add</strong> captures both kinds of pending material that Note has recognized. This lesson isolates automation: do not play new pads. Only move the knob, then <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 2.0.1 later lets you capture FX automation on existing clips or while recording audio. That is Module 9.5 / 8.4.10. The baseline rule stays: transport running → dotted line → <strong>Add</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["With transport running, move one effect knob until Clip Preview shows a dotted line.","Do not play new notes.","Tap <strong>Add</strong> at the bottom of the app.","Confirm the line is no longer only a pending dotted gesture (see 8.4.4 for the solid state).","Let the clip loop once without touching the knob.","Repeat on a second parameter from memory: move, see dotted, tap <strong>Add</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which control captures pending automation? <strong>Answer:</strong> The <strong>Add</strong> button at the bottom of the app.","What must you see first? <strong>Answer:</strong> A dotted line in Clip Preview (transport running).","Is <strong>Add</strong> only for notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Manual §7.5 uses <strong>Add</strong> to capture automation into the clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 (tap Add to capture automation)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one filter or reverb move into the clip you will keep for Gate H. Listen to one full loop without touching the knob."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-3","title":"8.4.3 — Multi-parameter capture in one Add","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.3 — Multi-parameter capture in one Add","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Move several parameters while transport runs, then tap <strong>Add</strong> once to capture all of them together."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5: “You can also adjust several parameters at the same time; when you press the <strong>Add</strong> button, automation will be captured for all of the parameters simultaneously.” One <strong>Add</strong> is a snapshot of every pending dotted line, not only the last knob you touched."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this when a section change is a combination: filter opens and reverb grows, or Saturator <strong>Base</strong> and Delay feedback climb together. You still need transport running. Each parameter you touch should show recognition in Clip Preview before you commit."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can mix device knobs and effect knobs in the same <strong>Add</strong>. You cannot capture automation for a parameter you never moved. If a line is not dotted, that parameter is not in the pending set."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the pass short enough to hear. A four-bar clip (you will use four bars in 8.4.11) is enough. Do not chase a perfect multi-knob performance on the first try — 8.4.6 lets you update."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Launch a looping clip. Transport running.","Move one effect knob until a dotted line appears.","Move a second knob (another FX parameter or a device control). Confirm more pending motion.","Tap <strong>Add</strong> once. Do not tap it per knob.","Let the clip loop and watch both values (8.4.5).","From memory: one <strong>Add</strong> captures every pending parameter at once."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many times do you tap <strong>Add</strong> for three pending knobs? <strong>Answer:</strong> Once. All pending automation captures together.","What must be true for each parameter? <strong>Answer:</strong> You adjusted it with transport running so Note recognized it (dotted line).","Can device and effect knobs share one <strong>Add</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Manual §7.5 covers device and audio-effect parameters."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 (several parameters at the same time; one Add)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a two-knob move on your Gate H clip: one tone control and one space control. Listen twice."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-4","title":"8.4.4 — A solid automation line means the automation is captured","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.4 — A solid automation line means the automation is captured","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Read Clip Preview and the parameter strip: solid line plus a dot under the name means the automation is stored."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5: “Captured automation appears as a solid line in the Clip Preview.” The dotted line from 8.4.1 is only recognition. After <strong>Add</strong>, the line goes solid. “To make it easier to recognize which parameters have been automated, a dot is added below the name of an automated parameter.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Two visuals, one fact:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Dotted line</strong> = pending, not in the clip yet","<strong>Solid line + dot under the name</strong> = captured"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"If you see a dot under a name you did not mean to automate, that parameter is already stored. Delete it in 8.4.8 rather than fighting the knob. If you see a dotted line after a later tweak, you are in the update path (8.4.6), not the first capture."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 2.0.1 changes how you open the parameter menu (tap the dial for Insert/Mute/Delete). The solid-line / dot language is still the manual’s capture proof."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Capture one parameter with <strong>Add</strong> if this clip has none.","Look at Clip Preview. Confirm the line is solid, not dotted.","Look under the parameter name. Confirm the dot.","Open a different tab that you did not automate. Confirm no extra dots.","Stop and relaunch the clip. The solid line and dot remain.","Recite: dotted = pending; solid + dot = stored."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a solid line in Clip Preview mean? <strong>Answer:</strong> The automation is captured into the clip.","What extra mark identifies an automated parameter? <strong>Answer:</strong> A dot below the parameter name.","What does a dotted line still mean after you already have other automation? <strong>Answer:</strong> New pending motion, not yet captured (update with <strong>Add</strong>)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 (solid line; dot below the name)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Point at every dotted versus solid line on your Gate H clip. Write the names that have dots."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-5","title":"8.4.5 — Playback shows automated values move","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.5 — Playback shows automated values move","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With transport running, watch captured knobs move by themselves and confirm the sound follows the solid line."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5: “When transport is running, you will also see the values of the automated parameters adjust automatically.” That moving knob is the playback proof. If the knob sits still and the line is solid, you are looking at the wrong parameter or transport is stopped."},{"type":"prose","html":"Hands off after you tap <strong>Add</strong>. Grabbing the knob writes new pending (dotted) automation. For this lesson you only watch. The musical reason: you are checking that the section change you meant is actually in the clip, not just in your last gesture."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is still clip automation, not a Live Arrangement envelope. It loops with the clip, up to the 16-bar clip limit. A four-bar clip repeats the same move every four bars unless you lengthen the clip or split scenes (8.4.12–8.4.13)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Launch the clip that has a solid automation line.","Keep your fingers off the automated knob.","Watch that knob (and its visual, if any) move as the clip loops.","Listen once with eyes closed, once while watching the knob.","Stop transport. Confirm the knob no longer animates.","Start transport again and point to the moving control from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When do automated values move on their own? <strong>Answer:</strong> When transport is running.","What should you do in this lesson besides watch? <strong>Answer:</strong> Nothing. Touching the knob creates new pending automation.","Does the move loop with the clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. It is clip automation, not an Arrangement timeline."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 (values adjust automatically when transport is running)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Watch one full loop of your Gate H automation. If the move is too short or too late, you will redraw it in 8.4.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-6","title":"8.4.6 — Re-adjust + Add to update automation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.6 — Re-adjust + Add to update automation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Replace captured automation by moving the same parameter again with transport running and tapping <strong>Add</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5: “You can continue to change parameters even after automation has been captured. When Note’s transport is running, you can adjust parameters again and new dotted lines will appear in the Clip Preview window. Tap the <strong>Add</strong> button to update the captured automation with the new values.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Update is not a separate button. It is the same path: transport on → new dotted line → <strong>Add</strong>. The new capture replaces what was stored for those parameters. Use this when 8.4.5 showed a move that is too fast, too late, or too deep."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you only wanted a straight hold at one value, use <strong>Insert</strong> (8.4.7) instead of performing another ramp. If you want the parameter static at the preset default, you must <strong>Delete</strong> first (8.4.8–8.4.9) because <strong>Return to Default</strong> is greyed while automated."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can update several parameters in one <strong>Add</strong>, same as 8.4.3."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Play a clip that already has a solid line.","With transport running, perform a clearer move on the same knob.","Confirm a new dotted line in Clip Preview.","Tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Watch one loop (hands off) and confirm the new shape.","Repeat once from memory if the update is still wrong."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you update existing automation? <strong>Answer:</strong> Transport running, adjust again (dotted line), tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Does update use a different button than first capture? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <strong>Add</strong> updates the captured values.","What if you want the factory default instead of a new ramp? <strong>Answer:</strong> Delete the automation first. <strong>Return to Default</strong> stays grey while automated."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5 Updating Existing Automation"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Update your Gate H move so the change is obvious in one loop and still musical. Stop after two updates."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-7","title":"8.4.7 — Parameter menu: Insert straight-line automation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.7 — Parameter menu: Insert straight-line automation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Insert a straight line of automation at the parameter’s current setting from the parameter options menu."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5.1: tap a parameter name to open the parameter options menu. <strong>Insert</strong> “adds a straight line of automation at the parameter’s current setting.” Use <strong>Insert</strong> when you want a held value — a filter parked open for the whole clip, a reverb stay-wet scene — without performing a ramp."},{"type":"prose","html":"Version gate the menu location:"},{"type":"list","items":["Local manual (pre-2.0 surface): tap the <strong>parameter name</strong>.","RN 2.0.1: tapping a parameter <strong>dial</strong> opens a context menu with Reset and Insert/Mute/Delete Automation. That menu replaces the parameter menu on the bottom half of the screen."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 1.0.15 had removed the <strong>Mute</strong> option for automation. RN 2.0.1 lists <strong>Mute</strong> again in the new dial menu. {type:'gap'} Sources do not describe what Mute does in 2.0.1. This lesson uses only <strong>Insert</strong>, which both surfaces document."},{"type":"prose","html":"Set the knob where you want it, then <strong>Insert</strong>. You should get a solid line and a dot without a dotted performance pass."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Pick a parameter that is not yet automated (or delete it first if it is).","Set the knob to the value you want held.","Open the parameter menu: tap the <strong>name</strong> (pre-2.0.1) or the <strong>dial</strong> (2.0.1+).","Choose <strong>Insert</strong>.","Confirm a solid line and a dot. Play the clip hands-off.","Insert a straight line on a second parameter from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>Insert</strong> write? <strong>Answer:</strong> A straight line of automation at the current setting.","Where do you open the menu? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the parameter name (manual). On 2.0.1+, tap the dial for the new context menu.","Do you need a dotted performance first? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <strong>Insert</strong> writes the straight line from the current value."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5.1 Insert","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Mute removed); 2.0.1 (dial context menu: Reset, Insert/Mute/Delete)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Insert a straight filter or wet value on a duplicate clip so you can compare a ramp (8.4.2) with a hold."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-8","title":"8.4.8 — Parameter menu: Delete automation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.8 — Parameter menu: Delete automation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Delete automation for one parameter from the parameter options menu and confirm the dot and solid line are gone."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5.1: <strong>Delete</strong> “removes any automation for the selected parameter.” It does not delete the clip or the notes. Only that parameter’s captured line is removed. After <strong>Delete</strong>, the dot under the name should go away, and <strong>Return to Default</strong> becomes available again (8.4.9)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Open the same menu as <strong>Insert</strong>: tap the parameter name (manual), or on 2.0.1+ tap the dial for the context menu that includes Insert/Mute/Delete. Delete is per parameter. Other automated knobs stay."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you meant to reshape the line, prefer 8.4.6 (re-adjust + <strong>Add</strong>) instead of delete-and-start-over. Use <strong>Delete</strong> when the parameter should be static, or when you automated the wrong knob in a multi-parameter <strong>Add</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"There is no source for an “undo automation only” command beyond general Undo/Redo in Session View. If you delete by mistake, try Undo immediately."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a parameter that shows a dot and a solid line.","Open the parameter options menu (name, or dial on 2.0.1+).","Choose <strong>Delete</strong>.","Confirm the dot is gone and Clip Preview no longer shows that solid line.","Play the clip. The knob should stay where you leave it.","Re-capture a short move, then delete it again from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>Delete</strong> remove? <strong>Answer:</strong> Automation for the selected parameter only — not the clip.","How do you open the command? <strong>Answer:</strong> Parameter options menu: tap the name (manual) or the dial (RN 2.0.1).","Do other automated parameters clear too? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Delete is per parameter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5.1 Delete","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (Insert/Mute/Delete on the dial menu)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Delete one experimental automation you do not want in Gate H. Leave the keeper line intact."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-9","title":"8.4.9 — Return to Default greyed when automated","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.9 — Return to Default greyed when automated","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Prove that <strong>Return to Default</strong> is unavailable on an automated parameter, then restore the default only after you delete the automation."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.5.1: “when a parameter is automated, the <strong>Return to Default</strong> option, which restores the parameter’s default value in the selected Track Preset, is grayed out.” RN 1.0.15 added <strong>Return to Default</strong> to the parameter page and double-tap reset. Automation wins: you cannot snap back to the preset default while a line owns that knob."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 2.0.1 puts <strong>Reset</strong> in the dial context menu next to Insert/Mute/Delete. {type:'gap'} The 2.0.1 note does not say whether <strong>Reset</strong> is also disabled while automated. On the manual surface, treat <strong>Return to Default</strong> as grey. If 2.0.1 <strong>Reset</strong> still fires on your build, record what you see; do not assume it clears automation."},{"type":"prose","html":"To get the factory value: <strong>Delete</strong> the automation, then <strong>Return to Default</strong> (or double-tap if that reset is not blocked). Double-tap on a fader still resets mixer volume (RN 1.0.15) and is a different control."},{"type":"prose","html":"The dot under the name is your warning. If you want a static factory sound, delete first."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Automate a parameter (<strong>Insert</strong> or <strong>Add</strong>) so the dot is visible.","Open the parameter options / parameter page.","Confirm <strong>Return to Default</strong> is grayed out.","<strong>Delete</strong> the automation.","Open the menu again and use <strong>Return to Default</strong>. Confirm the preset value returns.","Recite: automated → default greyed; delete first, then default."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why is <strong>Return to Default</strong> grey? <strong>Answer:</strong> The parameter is automated. Manual §7.5.1.","What does <strong>Return to Default</strong> restore when it is available? <strong>Answer:</strong> The parameter’s default value in the selected Track Preset.","What must you do before you can restore that default? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Delete</strong> the automation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5.1 (Return to Default grayed out when automated)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Return to Default; double-tap reset); 2.0.1 (Reset in dial menu)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a spare parameter, automate, prove the grey, delete, restore default. Then leave your Gate H automation untouched."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-10","title":"8.4.10 — Capture FX automation on existing clips","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.10 — Capture FX automation on existing clips","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put FX automation on a clip that already has notes, and version-gate the 2.0.1 “capture while recording” extra."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Baseline (Manual §7.5): the clip must exist and play. You do not need to recapture the MIDI. Launch the existing clip, move FX knobs, tap <strong>Add</strong>. That is how factory loops and older sketches get motion without rewriting notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"RN 2.0.1 adds a related line under Audio Tracks: “Capture FX Automation on existing clips or automate while recording.” Two readings you can defend:"},{"type":"list","items":["The same culture — existing clips still take FX automation via <strong>Add</strong>.","On 2.0.1+ audio tracks, you can also write FX automation during an audio record pass (Module 9.5.13)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not blend those. Pre-2.0 you automate MIDI clips’ device/FX parameters while they play. 2.0.1 extends the idea to audio recording. This lesson stays on an existing MIDI clip. If you only have 2.x audio clips, the same dotted → <strong>Add</strong> proof still applies once that clip is playing."},{"type":"prose","html":"FAQ still says warping is not available on the Sampler/Drum Rack path. That FAQ line is not about automation. You can automate Transpose on a sampler clip; pitch and speed stay coupled (Level 9)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that already contains notes and has no automation (or delete extras).","Launch it. Do not recapture the MIDI.","Move an <strong>FX1</strong> or <strong>FX2</strong> knob. Confirm the dotted line.","Tap <strong>Add</strong>. Confirm solid line + dot.","If you are on 2.0.1+, read the RN line and note that “while recording” waits for Module 9.5.","Play the clip hands-off and confirm the notes and the FX move together."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Must you recapture notes to add FX automation? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Automate while the existing clip plays, then <strong>Add</strong>.","What did RN 2.0.1 add? <strong>Answer:</strong> Capture FX automation on existing clips, or automate while recording (audio-track context).","Does sampler Transpose automation uncouple speed? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. FAQ: transpose still affects playback speed on the sampler path."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.5","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (Capture FX Automation on existing clips or while recording)","Note FAQ (automation via Capture; warp/transpose on sampler path)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Add one FX automation pass to a clip you captured in an earlier level. Do not change the notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-11","title":"8.4.11 — filter open over 4 bars","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.11 — filter open over 4 bars","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture an Auto Filter (or other filter) opening across a 4-bar clip so the loop becomes a section rise."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You already know the capture path. This lab fixes length and target. Clips can be up to 16 bars (FAQ / RN 1.0.15). Use <strong>4 bars</strong> so the open is readable in one loop and still short enough to update."},{"type":"prose","html":"Prefer <strong>Auto Filter</strong> in <strong>FX1</strong> (manual: analog-style filter with LFO and envelope; device since 1.4). If your version lacks Auto Filter, use a filter control the instrument already shows (Drum Sampler Filter, Melodic Sampler <strong>Filter Freq</strong>). {type:'gap'} Sources do not name Auto Filter’s cutoff knob. Move the control that clearly opens the tone."},{"type":"prose","html":"Transport running. Start closed, open across the four bars, tap <strong>Add</strong>. Watch the solid line. If the open finishes in bar 1, update (8.4.6). If you only need it parked open, <strong>Insert</strong> is the wrong tool for this lab — you want a ramp."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep notes unchanged. The filter is the section."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set or recapture the clip at <strong>4 bars</strong> (Add Clip 4, or crop/duplicate via the loop brace as you already learned).","Load <strong>Auto Filter</strong> in <strong>FX1</strong> if version ≥ 1.4.","Launch the clip. Start the filter closed.","Open it across the four bars. Tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Listen hands-off. Update once if the rise is early or late.","Recite: 4-bar clip, transport on, ramp, <strong>Add</strong>, solid line."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why 4 bars? <strong>Answer:</strong> Long enough to hear a rise, inside the 16-bar clip limit, easy to loop as a section.","What device does this lab prefer? <strong>Answer:</strong> Auto Filter (1.4+). Fallback: the instrument’s own filter control.","What proves the ramp is stored? <strong>Answer:</strong> Solid line in Clip Preview and a dot under the parameter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Auto Filter; §7.5 automation; §7.2.2–7.2.3 instrument filters","Note Release Notes 1.4 (Auto Filter); 1.0.15 (clips up to 16 bars)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep this 4-bar filter clip. You will contrast it with a wet ride and a delay climb in the next two labs."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-12","title":"8.4.12 — reverb send-ish wet ride for scene transition","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.12 — reverb send-ish wet ride for scene transition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ride a Reverb amount so one scene stays drier and the next scene blooms — using <strong>Pad FX Send Amount</strong> on drums or the wet-style control Reverb actually shows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note has no Live return track. “Send-ish” means the documented send you do have: <strong>Send Amount</strong> on <strong>Pad FX</strong> (Manual §7.4), or the wet-style mix control on a Reverb loaded in <strong>FX2</strong> / <strong>Kit FX</strong> / Main. {type:'gap'} The manual does not label a Reverb Dry/Wet knob. If you see a mix control, ride that. On a Drum Rack, ride <strong>Send Amount</strong> on the snare or clap."},{"type":"prose","html":"Scenes are rows. Tap a scene number to launch every clip in that row (Manual §4.3). Duplicate the clip into the next scene (long-press clip → duplicate, or Duplicate Clip in Instrument View). Automate the first clip drier and the second wetter, or automate one longer clip if you prefer a single rise. Max 8 scenes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking extract: intro/outro and section changes matter; long reverb can be a shot. Use the ride as a transition, not as a permanent wash on the kick."},{"type":"prose","html":"Main Reverb (1.0.15+) affects the whole Set. Prefer per-track Reverb so the drums can stay dry."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Put Reverb in <strong>Pad FX</strong> or <strong>FX2</strong>.","Duplicate the clip into the next scene, or prepare two scenes with the same part.","On the first-scene clip, capture a low send/wet line (<strong>Insert</strong> or a small <strong>Add</strong>).","On the second-scene clip, capture a higher send/wet ride with transport running, then <strong>Add</strong>.","Tap the first scene number, then the second. Hear the bloom as the scene changes.","From memory: Note’s send is <strong>Send Amount</strong> on <strong>Pad FX</strong>, not a Live return."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What official control is the “send” on drums? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Send Amount</strong> on <strong>Pad FX</strong>.","How do you launch the transition? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the scene number to launch the row.","Should you put this wash on Main for the lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> Prefer per-track Reverb so the rest of the Set can stay dry."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Send Amount / Reverb; §4.3 Scenes; §7.5","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (sections; long reverb shot)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Main FX — optional, not required here)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave scene 1 drier and scene 2 wetter. That pair is one of your Gate H section changes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l8-4-13","title":"8.4.13 — Delay feedback climb into next scene","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"8.4.13 — Delay feedback climb into next scene","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 8 · Module 8.4 — Automation complete</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Climb Delay <strong>Feedback</strong> on the last loop of a scene so repeats spill into the next scene, then settle."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Manual §7.4: Delay has time and filter modulation. RN 1.2.1: <strong>Time</strong> and <strong>Feedback</strong> show before the filter section. <strong>Feedback</strong> is the sourced knob this lab rides. Too much feedback piles up forever; use a clip-length climb, not a stuck maximum."},{"type":"prose","html":"Put Delay in <strong>FX2</strong> so it repeats the already-shaped tone from <strong>FX1</strong>. Capture a low feedback line on the body of the scene, then a climb on a transition clip — or update one clip so the last cycle is the climb (8.4.6). Launch the next scene (scene number) while the repeats are still decaying. Scene count max is 8."},{"type":"prose","html":"If 2.0.1 Delay LFO options are present, you may hear extra motion. Do not require those options. The lab is the <strong>Feedback</strong> climb."},{"type":"prose","html":"Stop the climb after the transition. A second clip with <strong>Insert</strong> at low feedback, or <strong>Delete</strong> plus a static low value, keeps the next section clean."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load <strong>Delay</strong> in <strong>FX2</strong>. Find <strong>Feedback</strong> (before the filter section on 1.2.1+).","On the outgoing clip, start feedback low. With transport running, raise it across one loop. Tap <strong>Add</strong>.","Prepare the next scene with the same or a reduced part.","Launch the next scene as the repeats swell.","On the incoming clip, <strong>Insert</strong> or capture low feedback so the pile-up ends.","Repeat the scene change from memory and listen for spill, then settle."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which Delay parameter does this lab automate? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Feedback</strong> (named in RN 1.2.1).","Where does Delay usually sit on a synth track? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>FX2</strong>, after tone in <strong>FX1</strong>.","How do you leave the next section clean? <strong>Answer:</strong> Incoming clip holds low feedback (<strong>Insert</strong> or a new <strong>Add</strong>), not the same climb."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §7.4 Delay; §4.3 Scenes; §7.5","Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (Time and Feedback before filter); 2.0.1 (optional LFO options)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Record (mentally) one successful scene handoff: climb, launch, settle. That is your third Gate H automation."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l8-4-13</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
